   ("Older streets, street parking, and the college",
    ["Watertown's older residential blocks were built before two-car garages, and a lot of the housing stock still relies on street parking. A street-parked car in a Wisconsin winter takes every plough pass and every freeze-thaw cycle with nothing between it and the weather, and it never gets to dry out. That is the never-garaged profile we see in Milwaukee, in a town a third the size.",
     "The university on the east side adds student vehicles: driven hard, cleaned rarely, and frequently sold or handed on at the end of a degree. A full reset before that handover is the cheapest thing that can be done to the resale number, and it is the most common Watertown booking we get from the college side of town."]),
   ("The Dodge County edge",
    ["North and west of the city it turns rural quickly, and the county roads into Dodge County carry the farm-season material \u2014 field mud, spreading, tractor traffic \u2014 that dries onto lower panels and into arches and does not rinse. A Watertown vehicle that does the corridor by day and a rural road home at night collects both the greasy freight film and the alkaline farm residue, which is an unusual combination and one that needs a degreasing stage and an organic stage before the standard decontamination."]),
