{
 "slug": "sussex-wi",
 "name": "Sussex",
 "county": "Waukesha County",
 "zip": "53089",
 "drive": "About 25 minutes",
 "lat": 43.1339, "lng": -88.2223,
 "title": "Auto Detailing for Sussex, WI | Sudz Up Detailing",
 "meta": "Auto detailing for Sussex, WI. New-construction dust, subdivision build traffic and new-home, new-car households. About 25 minutes from Hartford. Call 414-286-1609.",
 "h1": "Auto Detailing for Sussex, Wisconsin",
 "lede": "Sussex has been building for years, and a village under construction does specific and predictable things to the vehicles living in it.",
 "glance": [("County", "Waukesha County"), ("ZIP", "53089"), ("From our shop", "~25 minutes"), ("Local factor", "New construction")],
 "body": [
   ("Living in a subdivision that is still being built",
    ["A large share of Sussex households are in developments where building is still going on around them, and construction traffic is a different exposure from ordinary suburban driving.",
     "Site vehicles track mud onto new streets, which dries into a fine clay dust that gets thrown up by every car that follows. Concrete and mortar work throws alkaline dust. Drywall work throws gypsum. All of it settles on paint, and gypsum in particular leaves a chalky film that bonds when it dries on damp paint and does not rinse.",
     "Inside the cabin it is worse, because that dust gets tracked in on shoes at every trip and it is pale enough to show on dark interiors within days. It works into seat stitching and vents, where a vacuum passes over it without lifting anything."]),
   ("New home, new car",
    ["New-construction households very frequently have new or nearly new vehicles, and that changes the right advice.",
     "A new car in a construction zone is taking damage from the day it arrives, before the owner has any reason to think about protection. Alkaline dust and clay film sitting on a new clear coat through a summer of dew cycles is how a two-year-old car ends up with a bonnet that looks five years old.",
     "Protection early is the answer here rather than cleaning later, and that is a conversation about coating and interior protection at the point of purchase rather than a reset down the line."]),
   ("New driveways and fresh landscaping",
    ["Newly poured concrete drives shed fine dust for months. Fresh asphalt transfers onto tyres and rocker panels. New landscaping means bare soil, mulch and fresh sod, all of which end up on shoes and in footwells while the yard is establishing.",
     "None of it is dramatic. All of it adds up over a first year in a new house, which is why we see so many Sussex vehicles arriving for a first proper reset about twelve months after the move."]),
   ("The Bugline Trail",
    ["The Bugline Recreation Trail runs through Sussex on the old rail bed toward Menomonee Falls and Lannon, and the village is a starting point for a lot of cyclists and walkers. That puts bike racks on Sussex vehicles, with the hitch-rack rub on tailgates and roof-rack drip lines that come with them, and it puts trail grit and chain lube on rear bumpers. Dogs walked on the trail get into cargo areas wet, and the moisture goes into the carpet backing where the bacteria that follow are the humid-day smell."]),
   ("Highway 164 and Highway 74",
    ["Sussex commutes on Highway 164 south to I-94 and east on Highway 74 toward Menomonee Falls, and both are treated state routes. A Sussex commuter takes state-highway brine across the whole vehicle in winter on top of the construction dust from the neighbourhood, which means the paint has alkaline mineral film and salt film bonded together by March \u2014 a combination that needs an alkaline-neutralising pre-wash before the standard iron and clay stages.",
     "Highway 164's stop-start through the village lights adds brake dust to wheels and lower panels."]),
   ("Lisbon farmland to the north",
    ["North of the village the Town of Lisbon is still working farmland, and the county roads out there carry the farm-season material that dries hard onto lower panels and into arches. A Sussex household in a new build on the northern edge gets construction dust from the subdivision, brine from 164, and farm residue from the road to the north, and those are three different contaminants that come off in three different stages."]),
   ("A Sussex year, and what to do first",
    ["For a new car in a new build: coat it in the first month, before the alkaline dust has had a season to bond. Interior protection at the same time, before the yard has had a year to grind into the footwells.",
     "For a vehicle that has already done a year: the twelve-month reset, then the protection. April is the month for the reset once 164 stops being treated; September for protection if the coating was not done at purchase.",
     "Sussex is about twenty-five minutes from 2948 WI-83 in Hartford on Highway 164 and WI-83. Full details are a day; interior work is usually same-day. The <a href=\"/services/full-detail/sussex-wi/\">twelve-month reset page</a> and the <a href=\"/services/ceramic-coating/sussex-wi/\">new-car coating page</a> cover the two Sussex-specific decisions."]),
   ("New streets in winter",
    ["A subdivision that is still being built out has streets that were poured last year, drives that were poured last month, and a lot of construction traffic that does not stop for snow. New subdivision streets are typically low on a village plough priority relative to the collector roads, so they are cleared later and stay icy longer, and site vehicles pack whatever is on them into ruts.",
     "For a household in a new build that means a car that starts every winter trip on an icy, rutted, lightly treated street, drives onto a heavily treated state route, and comes home to a drive that may still be shedding concrete dust. The brine from 164 and the alkaline dust from the street land together on damp paint, and by March they are one bonded film. The April reset is where that comes off, and the coating in the first month is what stops it forming the following winter."]),
 ],
 "faq": [
   ("Why does my new car already look chalky?", "Construction dust \u2014 gypsum, mortar and concrete residue \u2014 bonds to paint when it dries on damp surfaces. It needs decontamination rather than washing, and on a new car the better answer is protection before it happens."),
   ("How far is Sussex from your shop?", "About twenty-five minutes to 2948 WI-83 in Hartford. Call or text 414-286-1609 to arrange a drop-off."),
   ("Is it worth coating a new car in a construction area?", "Usually more than anywhere else, because the exposure starts on day one. A coated surface releases alkaline dust and clay film with a rinse instead of letting it bond."),
   ("We just moved into a new build with a new car. What first?", "Coat the car in the first month and protect the interior at the same time. The construction dust starts bonding on day one, and the job is smallest before it has."),
   ("Does the Bugline Trail affect my car?", "Bike racks rub bumpers and leave drip lines on the roof, and a dog off the trail gets into the cargo area wet. Rack marks are a correction job; the dog is fabric protection before the season."),
   ("Why does my new street stay icy longer than the main road?", "New subdivision streets are usually low on the plough priority relative to collector roads, so they are cleared later and stay icy longer, and site traffic packs whatever is on them into ruts. It means more time on lightly treated, rutted pavement at the start and end of every winter trip."),
 ],
},
